    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1920, Ulster Park joined New York’s first fruit grower cooperative, pioneering apple production in the Hudson Valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/ulster_park_new_york"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The hamlet’s name comes from Dutch “Klyne Esopus,” meaning “Little Esopus,” reflecting its origins in the 17th‑century Dutch colony.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/ulster_park_new_york"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Ulster Park sits on the west bank of the Hudson River, about five miles south of Kingston, giving it a scenic riverside setting.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/ulster_park_new_york"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Camp Chi‑Wan‑Da, a historic Jewish summer camp, once hosted future Hall of Fame pitcher Sandy Koufax during his youth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/ulster_park_new_york"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Hudson Valley’s humid continental climate brings warm summers and snowy winters, with average annual precipitation around 45 inches.",
